      ":question/id": "9143ec8b-3e51-5f1b-9339-05b3bf05bf62",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 2 u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=- t^{2} - t$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)=f′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 e^{u}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(- 2 u^{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u} \\\\ - 4 u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ u^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- t^{2} - t\\right)=- 2 t - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(u(t) \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{u(t)} \\\\ - 4 u(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(u^{′}(t)\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cos{\\left(t^{2} + t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{- t^{2} - t} \\\\ 4 t^{2} + 4 t\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\left(- 2 t - 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(- 4 t - 2\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\left(t + 1\\right) \\right)} \\\\ 2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{- t \\left(t + 1\\right)} \\\\ - 4 t \\left(t + 1\\right) \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
